# From Predication To Kinematic Space

This chapter is the first book of the curriculum "Introduction to Informational
Mathematics." It is a route, not a replacement for the phase books. The route is:

```text
  \mathrm{Mark}
    \longrightarrow \mathrm{Utterance}
    \longrightarrow \mathrm{Articulating}
    \longrightarrow \mathrm{PotentialCell}
    \longrightarrow \mathrm{PredicationCell}
    \longrightarrow \mathrm{ArticulationChain}
    \longrightarrow \mathrm{DiamondPoint}
    \longrightarrow \mathrm{HoleDiamondKinematicSpace}
    \longrightarrow \mathrm{MarkStepDiamondBeads}.
```

Each arrow in that display is generated by a named carrier already present in
Lean. The reader should treat the formula as a dependency spine: no object to the
right is available until the object to its left has been generated.

The claim-site carried here is:

* curriculum: Introduction to Informational Mathematics;
* chapter: From Predication to Kinematic Space;
* generating sites: marks, utterances, articulating, potential cells,
  predication cells, articulation chains, diamonds, sequencing, bead threads;
* Lean carriers: `Mark`, `Utterance`, `Articulating`, `PredicationCell`,
  `CausalSet`, `DiamondPoint`, `HoleDiamondKinematicSpace`,
  `MarkStepDiamondBeads`;
* familiar comparison: syntax, directed graph, causal set, kinematic space,
  woven region;
* danger: reading kinematic space as imported spacetime rather than as the
  sequencing order generated from articulation.

The governing sentence is:

> Marks do not live in space; articulation threads marks into causal diamonds,
> and only then does kinematic space become a lawful carrier.

## Step 1: Mark

The floor is the mark:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Mark}

Technically, a mark is just a finite surface presentation:

```text
  m : \mathrm{Mark},
  \qquad
  \mathrm{surface}(m) : \mathrm{String}.
```

The formula is intentionally poor. A mark has no subject, no object, no truth
value, no causal position, and no neighborhood. The only typed distinction at
this level is positional kind:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Kind}

The two positions are token and claim:

```lean
example (k : Kind) : k = Kind.token \/ k = Kind.claim :=
  Kind.two_kinds k

example : Kind.token = Kind.claim -> False :=
  Kind.token_ne_claim
```

This is why the first formula is not \(m \in X\) for a pre-existing space. There
is no \(X\) yet.

## Step 2: Utterance

An utterance is the first structured carrier:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Utterance}

The formula is:

```text
  u = (t,c),
  \qquad
  t : \mathrm{Mark}_{\mathrm{token}},
  \qquad
  c : \mathrm{Mark}_{\mathrm{claim}}.
```

In Lean this is the ordered pair of fields `token` and `claim`, read by position:

```lean
example (u : Utterance) : u.at_ Kind.token = u.token.mark :=
  Utterance.at_token u

example (u : Utterance) : u.at_ Kind.claim = u.claim.mark :=
  Utterance.at_claim u
```

The order is not grammatical subject and predicate yet. It is only the
two-sided carrier from which articulation can proceed.

## Step 3: Articulation

Articulating is the last admitted recursion:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Articulating}

The recursive formula is:

```text
  a ::= \mathrm{utter}(u) \mid \mathrm{pair}(u,a).
```

The depth and floor of an articulation are generated by this recursion:

```text
  \mathrm{depth}(\mathrm{utter}(u)) = 0,
  \qquad
  \mathrm{depth}(\mathrm{pair}(u,a)) = \mathrm{depth}(a)+1,
  \qquad
  \mathrm{floor}(\mathrm{utter}(u)) = u.
```

Checked:

```lean
example (u : Utterance) :
    (Articulating.utter u).floor = u :=
  Articulating.floor_utter u

example (u : Utterance) (a : Articulating) :
    (Articulating.pair u a).moved = true :=
  Articulating.moved_pair u a
```

The important point for this curriculum is directionality. The constructor
`pair` continues an articulation. It does not recover an unarticulated state.

## Step 4: Potential Cell

Articulation has a four-corner cell because the utterance has two positions and
each position may be articulated or not:

```text
  \mathrm{corner}(b_t,b_c) \in
    \{\mathrm{potential},\mathrm{active},\mathrm{absurd},\mathrm{stable}\},
  \qquad
  (b_t,b_c) \in \mathbf{2}\times\mathbf{2}.
```

The typed initial source for predication is
{name Obsmetrologia.Articulating.PotentialCell}`Articulating.PotentialCell`.

It is the utterance before either side has been articulated:

```lean
example (u : Utterance) :
    (Articulating.PotentialCell.initial u).toCell.corner
      = Articulating.Corner.potential :=
  Articulating.PotentialCell.toCell_corner
    (Articulating.PotentialCell.initial u)
```

This is the gate into predication. Predication does not start from an arbitrary
triple; it starts from the potential articulation cell.

## Step 5: Predication

Predication is the first oriented predication cell. In Lean the carrier is
{name Obsmetrologia.PredicationCell}`PredicationCell`.

The seed formula is:

```text
  \operatorname{seed}(u)
    =
  (\operatorname{token}(u),\operatorname{claim}(u),\operatorname{token}(u)).
```

Lean records exactly that:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.PredicationCell.seed}

```lean
example (u : Utterance) :
    PredicationCell.seed u =
      ⟨u.token, u.claim, u.token⟩ :=
  rfl
```

The triple \((s,p,o)\) is lawful only now:

```text
  \varphi = (s,p,o)
  \quad\text{with}\quad
  s,o : \mathrm{Entity},
  \quad
  p : \mathrm{Predicate}.
```

Converse and specialization are therefore operations on generated positions, not
ambient operations assumed before the phase:

```lean
example (u : Utterance) :
    PredicationCell.converse (PredicationCell.seed u)
      = PredicationCell.seed u :=
  PredicationCell.converse_seed u

example
    (f g : Predicate -> Predicate)
    (phi : PredicationCell) :
    PredicationCell.specialize f
        (PredicationCell.specialize g phi)
      = PredicationCell.specialize (f ∘ g) phi :=
  PredicationCell.specialize_comp f g phi
```

This is the end of the language-side part of the route. The next move does not
add an external spacetime. It reads an actual articulation as a finite causal
chain.

## Step 6: Articulation Chain

One `Articulating` inhabitant carries its own list of prefixes:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.Causal.ArticulationChain.prefixes}

The route from recursion to causal order is:

```text
  \operatorname{prefixes}(\mathrm{utter}(u))
    = [\mathrm{utter}(u)],
  \qquad
  \operatorname{prefixes}(\mathrm{pair}(u,a))
    = \operatorname{prefixes}(a)\mathbin{++}[\mathrm{pair}(u,a)].
```

Direct continuation is the edge relation:

```text
  x \prec_0 y
  \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
  y = \mathrm{pair}(u,x)
  \quad\text{for some utterance }u.
```

Lean proves that the settled causal order is prefix membership:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.Causal.ArticulationChain.causalLe_iff_mem_prefixes}

Checked:

```lean
example (a x y : Articulating) :
    CausalLe (causalSet a) x y
      ↔ x ∈ prefixes y :=
  causalLe_iff_mem_prefixes a x y
```

This is the first kinematic hint: order is no longer only grammatical. It is a
causal order generated by the continuation of articulation.

## Step 7: A Mark-Built Diamond

A single mark-built articulation step supplies a concrete causal diamond:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.markStepArticulating}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.markStepDiamondPoint}

The formula is:

```text
  u(t,c) = (t,c),
  \qquad
  a^+ = \mathrm{pair}(u(t,c),a),
  \qquad
  D(t,c,a) = (a,a^+).
```

The lower endpoint is the previous articulation tail, and the upper endpoint is
the paired articulation:

```lean
example (token claim : Mark) (tail : Articulating) :
    (markStepDiamondPoint token claim tail).lower.point
      = tail :=
  markStepDiamondPoint_lower_point token claim tail

example (token claim : Mark) (tail : Articulating) :
    (markStepDiamondPoint token claim tail).upper.point
      =
      markStepArticulating token claim tail :=
  markStepDiamondPoint_upper_point token claim tail
```

The diamond is not drawn over a background. Its endpoints are actual
articulations in the prefix causal set.

## Step 8: Kinematic Space

A diamond point is a pair of listed causal events with a strict reachability
witness:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.DiamondPoint}

The wrong relation is nesting:

```text
  (a,b) \preceq_{\mathrm{nest}} (a',b')
    \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
  a' \leq a \ \wedge\ b \leq b'.
```

Nesting self-loops, so it cannot be the causal-set order:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.nesting_self_edge}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.nesting_no_strictOrderClosure_of_point}

The lawful relation is sequencing:

```text
  (a,b) \prec_{\mathrm{seq}} (a',b')
    \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
  b \leq a'.
```

The kinematic space is therefore the causal set of diamond points ordered by
sequencing:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.HoleDiamondKinematicSpace}

Checked:

```lean
example {E : Type} (C : CausalSet E)
    [CausalLeDecision C]
    [DecidableRel C.reaches] :
    CausalSet (DiamondPoint C) :=
  HoleDiamondKinematicSpace C
```

This is the exact point where "kinematic space" becomes lawful in the
curriculum: not at Mark, not at Utterance, not even at Predication, but after
articulation has generated causal diamonds and sequencing has supplied the
strict order.

## Step 9: Woven Regions

The woven reading begins when the sequencing relation is read as a bead thread.
The thread is still not arbitrary graph data:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.sequencingBeadAdjacency}

The canonical bead-adjacency tuple is:

```text
  (\mathrm{thread},\mathrm{contact},\mathrm{cell},\mathrm{visible})
    =
  (\prec_{\mathrm{seq}},\prec_{\mathrm{seq}},
    \prec_{\mathrm{seq}},\prec_{\mathrm{seq}}).
```

For a mark-built step, the bead carrier is the diamond-point carrier over the
mark-generated causal set:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.MarkStepDiamondBeads.threadCausalSet}

and its direct edge relation is sequencing:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.MarkStepDiamondBeads.threadCausalSet_prec0}

Checked:

```lean
example (token claim : Mark) (tail : Articulating)
    (P : MarkStepDiamondBeads.Payload token claim tail) :
    (MarkStepDiamondBeads.threadCausalSet
        token claim tail P).prec0
      =
      sequencingPrec0
        (markStepCausalSet token claim tail) :=
  MarkStepDiamondBeads.threadCausalSet_prec0
    token claim tail P
```

This is what "woven region" means at this point in the course. It is not cloth
metaphor and not a new primitive space. It is the bead layer whose thread
adjacency is the already-proved sequencing relation on diamond points. The
remaining bead payload supplies material realization data, but the kinematic
thread itself is generated from marks through articulation.

## The Full Route

The route can now be read as the following chain of definitions:

```text
\begin{aligned}
  m &:\mathrm{Mark}, \\
  u &= (m_{\mathrm{token}},m_{\mathrm{claim}}), \\
  a^+ &= \mathrm{pair}(u,a), \\
  \varphi(u) &= (\operatorname{token}(u),\operatorname{claim}(u),
    \operatorname{token}(u)), \\
  C(a^+) &= (\operatorname{prefixes}(a^+),\prec_0), \\
  D(u,a) &= (a,a^+) \in \mathrm{DiamondPoint}(C(a^+)), \\
  K(C) &= \mathrm{HoleDiamondKinematicSpace}(C), \\
  B(t,c,a,P) &= \mathrm{MarkStepDiamondBeads.threadCausalSet}(t,c,a,P).
\end{aligned}
```

The curriculum point is that each line earns a word:

* `Mark`: admission;
* `Utterance`: positional pairing;
* `Articulating`: recursive continuation;
* `PredicationCell`: oriented predication cell;
* `causalSet`: prefix order;
* `DiamondPoint`: interval event;
* `HoleDiamondKinematicSpace`: sequencing-ordered diamond space;
* `MarkStepDiamondBeads`: woven region over that sequencing thread.

## Repair Path

If this chapter fails for a reader, use this route:

1. Read Book II, "Genesis Boundary," for marks, utterances, articulating, and the
   potential cell.
2. Read Book III, "Predication As Position," for seed predication and the
   subject-predicate-object triple.
3. Read Book VIII, "Kinematic Points, Regions, And Patches" for the
   diamond-point and kinematic-region bridge.
4. Read Book VIII, "Hole Shroud," from "Diamond Kinematic Space" through the
   kinematic theorem.
5. Read the prefix-order theorem
   {name Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.Causal.ArticulationChain.causalLe_iff_mem_prefixes}`causalLe_iff_mem_prefixes`.
6. Read the bead-thread theorem
   {name Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.MarkStepDiamondBeads.threadCausalSet}`MarkStepDiamondBeads.threadCausalSet`.

